Q: JWT C++ OpenSSL RS256 Signature Verification A: <p>The problem is that <code>OSSL_PARAM_construct_BN()</code> uses the native byte order of the underlying machine architecture for representation (see <a href="https://docs.openssl.org/4.0/man3/OSSL_PARAM/#ossl_param-fields" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>OSSL_PARAM</code></a> and the <code>OSSL_PARAM_UNSIGNED_INTEGER</code> section). On my machine, this is little-endian, which causes the raw key bytes to be inverted. This can easily be verified by exporting the generated <code>PKEY</code> as SPKI-PEM.</p>
<p>To avoid this, it makes more sense to follow the recommendation from the man pages and use <code>OSSL_PARAM_BLD_to_param()</code> instead (see <a href="https://docs.openssl.org/4.0/man3/OSSL_PARAM_BLD/#example-2" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>OSSL_PARAM_BLD</code></a>):</p>
<pre><code>...
OSSL_PARAM_BLD* bld = OSSL_PARAM_BLD_new(); 
OSSL_PARAM* params = NULL; 

BIGNUM* n = BN_bin2bn((const unsigned char*)rsa_n.data(), rsa_n.size(), NULL); 
BIGNUM* e = BN_bin2bn((const unsigned char*)rsa_e.data(), rsa_e.size(), NULL); 

if (bld == NULL 
    || !OSSL_PARAM_BLD_push_BN(bld, &quot;n&quot;, n) 
    || !OSSL_PARAM_BLD_push_BN(bld, &quot;e&quot;, e) 
    || (params = OSSL_PARAM_BLD_to_param(bld)) == NULL) 
    return -1; 
OSSL_PARAM_BLD_free(bld); 
/* Use params */
... 
OSSL_PARAM_free(params); 
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>Furthermore, the code can be optimized. OpenSSL provides the <a href="https://docs.openssl.org/4.0/man3/EVP_DigestVerifyInit/" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>EVP_DigestVerifyXXX()</code></a> family, which hashes implicitly. This simplifies the code to:</p>
<pre><code>...
EVP_MD_CTX* mdctx = EVP_MD_CTX_new(); 
EVP_DigestVerifyInit(mdctx, NULL, EVP_sha256(), NULL, pkey); 
int v = EVP_DigestVerify(mdctx 
    , (const unsigned char*)t_sig.data() 
    , t_sig.size() 
    , (const unsigned char*)t_dat.data() 
    , t_dat.size()); 
...
</code></pre>

Q: Chrome PWA no longer calculating dvh properly on initial load A: <p>I had the same issue in my PWA, and the following code in the Vue layout component helped me.</p>
<pre><code>&lt;script setup&gt;
onMounted(() =&gt; {
   const mainElement = document.querySelector('main')

   const dvh = window.visualViewport ? window.visualViewport.height : window.innerHeight
   mainElement.style.minHeight = `${dvh}px`
})
&lt;/script&gt;

&lt;template&gt;
      &lt;main class=&quot;min-h-dvh flex flex-col&quot;&gt;
         &lt;div class=&quot;flex-1 flex flex-col&quot;&gt;
            &lt;router-view /&gt;
         &lt;/div&gt;
      &lt;/main&gt;
&lt;/template&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: What&#39;s the best way to remove an item from signal form array? A: <p>If this works, then your solution is fine. What you want to avoid is mutating the original array monitored by the signal. I answered a similar <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/q/79962730/3092298">question</a> a while back, and an alternative is to use the <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/toSpliced" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Array.toSpliced()</a> method:</p>
<pre><code>  removeItem(index: number): void {
    const newOrderModel = this.orderModel().items.toSpliced(index, 1);

    this.orderModel.set(newOrderModel);
  }
</code></pre>
<p>If there is any advantage to using <code>toSpliced()</code> it's that the code is shorter. Better? Debatable.</p>
